{
    "map": {
        "createnetworkpaths.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies whether complete, and possibly overlapping, paths from the destinations to the sources are calculated or if nonoverlapping network paths are created.</p><ul><li>Unchecked—Complete paths from the destinations to the sources are calculated, which can be overlapping. This is the default.</li><li>Checked—Nonoverlapping network paths are calculated.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "createnetworkpaths.title": "Create Network Paths",
        "description": "Calculates the optimal path from a source to a destination as a line.",
        "destinationfield.descr": "<xdoc><p>The field that will be used to obtain values for the destination locations.</p><p>This field must be an integer.</p></xdoc>",
        "destinationfield.title": "Destination Field",
        "inputbackdirectionraster.descr": "<xdoc><p>The back direction raster contains calculated directions in degrees. The direction identifies the next cell along the optimal path back to the least accumulative cost source while avoiding barriers.</p><p>The range of values is from 0 degrees to 360 degrees. The value 0 is reserved for the source cells. Due east (right) is 90 degrees, and the values increase clockwise (180 is south, 270 is west, and 360 is north).</p></xdoc>",
        "inputbackdirectionraster.title": "Input Back Direction or Flow Direction Raster",
        "inputdestinationrasterorfeatures.descr": "<xdoc><p>A raster or feature dataset identifying locations from which the least accumulative cost path is determined to the least costly source.</p><p>For a raster, the input type must be integer, and it must consist of cells that have valid values (zero is a valid value). The remaining cells must be assigned NoData. For a feature service, the input type can be point, line or polygon.</p></xdoc>",
        "inputdestinationrasterorfeatures.title": "Input Raster or Feature Destinations",
        "inputdistanceaccumulationraster.descr": "<xdoc><p>The distance accumulation raster is used to determine the optimal path from the sources to the destinations.</p><p>The distance accumulation raster is usually created with the Distance Accumulation or Distance Allocation tool. Each cell in the distance accumulation raster represents the minimum accumulative cost distance over a surface from each cell to a set of source cells.</p></xdoc>",
        "inputdistanceaccumulationraster.title": "Input Distance Accumulation Raster",
        "outputpolylinefeatures.title": "Output Polyline Feature",
        "outputpolylinename.descr": "The name of the output feature service that contains the optimal paths.",
        "outputpolylinename.title": "Output Optimal Path as Line Name",
        "pathtype.descr": "<xdoc><p>Specifies a keyword defining the manner in which the values and zones in the input destination data will be interpreted in the cost path calculations.</p><ul><li><span title=\"EACH_ZONE\">Each zone</span>—For each zone in the input destination data, a least-cost path will be determined and saved on the output raster. With this option, the least-cost path for each zone begins at the cell with the lowest cost distance weighting in the zone. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"BEST_SINGLE\">Best single</span>—For all cells in the input destination data, the least-cost path will be derived from the cell with the minimum of the least-cost paths to source cells.</li><li><span title=\"EACH_CELL\">Each cell</span>—For each cell with valid values in the input destination data, a least-cost will be is determined and saved on the output raster. With this option, each cell of the input destination data is treated separately, and a least-cost path is determined for each cell.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "pathtype.domain.BEST_SINGLE": "Best single",
        "pathtype.domain.EACH_CELL": "Each cell",
        "pathtype.domain.EACH_ZONE": "Each zone",
        "pathtype.title": "Path Type",
        "title": "Optimal Path As Line"
    }
}
